{
 "segments": [
  {
   "text": "Case Twenty-Three.",
   "start": 0.75,
   "end": 1.987,
   "pause": 0.6
  },
  {
   "text": "The Odd Glove.",
   "start": 2.5869999999999997,
   "end": 3.675,
   "pause": 1.4
  },
  {
   "text": "It was a bitter, windy evening, the kind when everyone in Tidewhistle Cove walks with their hands deep in their pockets.",
   "start": 5.075,
   "end": 12.35,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Morwenna Day had been growing a rare white orchid on the back windowsill of her flower shop for three years, and it had just bloomed for the very first time. At closing time she went to the front to lock up, and when she returned to the back, the window was open and the orchid, pot and all, was gone.",
   "start": 13.2,
   "end": 31.227,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Under the open window, on the cobbles outside, lay a single knitted glove. It was navy blue, for a right hand, with a pattern of little white anchors around the wrist.",
   "start": 32.077,
   "end": 42.53,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Agnes Bell was passing on her way home, and Morwenna called her in. Together they looked up and down the lane. Three people were nearby.",
   "start": 43.38,
   "end": 51.913,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Kerensa Hale was waiting at the bus stop, wearing a pair of navy gloves. They were plain, with no pattern at all, and she had one on each hand.",
   "start": 52.763,
   "end": 61.681,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Mr Fenwick was locking up the grocer's next door. He wasn't wearing any gloves at all, and his fingers were red with the cold. \"Never wear them,\" he said. \"Can't count change in gloves.\"",
   "start": 62.531,
   "end": 73.795,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "The third was Mr Lanyon, a visitor, walking briskly down the lane. On his left hand he wore a navy knitted glove with a pattern of little white anchors around the wrist. His right hand was pushed deep into his coat pocket.",
   "start": 74.645,
   "end": 88.725,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "\"Lost the other glove weeks ago,\" said Mr Lanyon, when Agnes asked. \"Haven't seen it since.\"",
   "start": 89.575,
   "end": 95.271,
   "pause": 1.1
  },
  {
   "text": "Can you solve it? Who took the orchid?",
   "start": 96.371,
   "end": 98.504,
   "pause": 1.2
  },
  {
   "text": "Think about it.",
   "start": 99.704,
   "end": 100.728,
   "pause": 0.9
  },
  {
   "text": "Take a moment, or pause here if you are listening. When you are ready, the solution follows.",
   "start": 101.628,
   "end": 106.855,
   "pause": 4.5
  },
  {
   "text": "The Solution.",
   "start": 111.355,
   "end": 112.379,
   "pause": 1.1
  },
  {
   "text": "Mr Lanyon took the orchid.",
   "start": 113.479,
   "end": 115.228,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "The glove under the window was for a right hand, navy blue, with a pattern of little white anchors. Mr Lanyon was wearing its partner, a navy left hand glove with exactly the same anchor pattern, and keeping his bare right hand in his pocket.",
   "start": 116.078,
   "end": 131.587,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Kerensa was wearing both of her gloves, and they were plain, with no pattern. Mr Fenwick doesn't wear gloves at all. Neither of them could have dropped a patterned right hand glove.",
   "start": 132.437,
   "end": 143.147,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Mr Lanyon had not lost the glove weeks ago. He had dropped it a few minutes earlier, while lifting the orchid through the window. The orchid turned up in a box on the back seat of his car, safe and sound. He was a passionate orchid grower and had driven three hundred miles to see it bloom. Morwenna let him take a cutting, properly, and he went home with the right glove back on his right hand.",
   "start": 143.997,
   "end": 166.29,
   "pause": 0.0
  }
 ],
